---
intent: >
  Desired outcome D3 (idempotency — all three artifacts already exist) is
  partially undermined: the orchestrator integration test still uses a
  platform-branching mcpEntry expectation that disagrees with the simplified
  mcpEntry() on non-Windows systems. Desired outcome D4 (after the CLI
  completes, a Claude Code session can invoke the aide MCP tools) is supported
  by the simplified entry shape — but the test that guards the entry shape in
  the orchestrator path would produce a false-passing result on Linux/macOS.
  Additionally, installAideTree has no unit test file, leaving it as the only
  helper in the installX family without isolated coverage.
misalignment:
  - test-gap
---

## Issues

- [x] `src/tools/init/index.test.ts` lines 22-24 — `expectedAideMcpEntry` still branches on `platform()`, computing `{ command: "npx", args: ["@aidemd-mcp/server"] }` on non-Windows. `mcpEntry()` now always returns the `cmd /c` form. On Linux/macOS the assertion on line 167 (`expect(mcpStep?.prescription?.entry).toEqual(expectedAideMcpEntry)`) passes by matching the wrong shape — the test silently validates a value that no longer exists. The line 220 fixture also seeds a `cmd /c`-less entry, which could cause the "already exists" detection to behave unexpectedly during test runs on non-Windows CI.
  Traces to: desired outcome D4 — after the CLI completes, a Claude Code session can invoke the aide MCP tools. The guard that ensures the always-cmd/c entry shape reaches the MCP planner is wrong on non-Windows.
  Misalignment: test-gap

- [x] `src/tools/init/installAideTree/index.test.ts` — file does not exist. `installAideTree` is the only helper in the `installX` family (`scaffoldCommands`, `installAgents`, `installSkills`) without a unit test file. Plan step 7 enumerated 7a–7e but did not include a test for this helper. The behavior — `exists` return when file present, `would-create` with canonical content when absent, `would-skip` when `readCanonicalDoc` throws — is untested at the unit level.
  Traces to: desired outcome D1 — running the CLI in a project with no existing config creates all three artifacts. The MCP-layer planner for the aide-tree artifact has no isolated coverage.
  Misalignment: test-gap

## Retro

Both issues are test-gaps that would have been caught earlier at the plan stage if the plan's step 7 had been written as a test checklist per file rather than per scenario. Step 7b said "remove the `platform` import from `wireMcp/index.test.ts`" — it named only that one test file. The parallel platform-branch in `src/tools/init/index.test.ts` was not named and was not updated. A plan step that names the specific assertion to change (not just the file) would have surfaced the omission. The `installAideTree` test gap arose because step 7 listed test files for the CLI-layer helpers (7a–7d) but skipped the MCP-layer counterpart — a consistent pattern would be one test-update item per new or modified helper, regardless of layer.
